questions.explanation:
Register a handler per method and path, parse the body with `express.json()`, and return the right status: `201` (plus `Location`) for create, `200` or `204` for update, `204` for delete, `404` if missing.

Why the others are wrong: Express does not parse bodies until you add `express.json()`, and returning 200 for everything hides created/empty/missing outcomes; `fetch` can send `PUT`, `DELETE` and `PATCH`, and writes over GET break caching and safety guarantees; and Express happily registers many handlers per path, one per method (`app.post`, `app.put`...).

```js
import express from "express";
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

app.post("/items", (req, res) => {
  const item = items.create(req.body);
  res.status(201).location(`/items/${item.id}`).json(item);
});
app.put("/items/:id", (req, res) => res.json(items.replace(req.params.id, req.body)));
app.delete("/items/:id", (req, res) => { items.remove(req.params.id); res.sendStatus(204); });
```

Register an error-handling middleware last; it has four parameters `(err, req, res, next)`. In Express 5 a rejected promise or thrown error in any handler reaches it. In Express 4 you must wrap async handlers and call `next(err)`.

Why the others are wrong: Express recognises error middleware by its four parameters, there is no `res.error` or `app.onError`; `app.listen` returns immediately, so its try/catch never sees request errors, and Express 5 kept error middleware; and error middleware must come after the routes, while Express 5 is exactly the version that forwards rejected promises without wrappers.

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  req.log?.error(err);
  res.status(err.status ?? 500).json({ error: { message: err.expose ? err.message : "Internal error" } });
});
```

Never trust `req.body`. Validate against a schema (Zod, Joi, Ajv) at the edge, return `400` or `422` with field errors, and pass only validated data inward.

Why the others are wrong: TypeScript types are erased at compile time and check nothing at runtime; ad hoc checks scattered through handlers miss cases, and silent coercion hides client bugs instead of returning a 400/422; and anyone can call the API directly, bypassing the front end, while database errors give poor messages and catch only some rules.

```js
import { z } from "zod";
const CreateItem = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1), price: z.number().positive() });
const parsed = CreateItem.safeParse(req.body);
if (!parsed.success) return res.status(422).json({ errors: parsed.error.flatten().fieldErrors });
```

Parse the body with a schema; on failure return `422` (or `400`) with a stable error shape (`code`, `message`, field `details`, ideally RFC 9457 problem details) that clients can rely on, and never send stack traces to clients. On success pass only the parsed data on and return `201` for a create.

Why the others are wrong: an unhandled Zod error becomes a 500 with internals instead of a client error with a stable shape; invalid input is a client error (400/422), 500 means the server failed; and stack traces leak implementation details to attackers and are useless to clients compared to field-level details.

```js
app.post("/items", (req, res) => {
  const r = CreateItem.safeParse(req.body);
  if (!r.success) {
    return res.status(422).json({
      error: { code: "VALIDATION_FAILED", message: "Invalid item", details: r.error.flatten().fieldErrors },
    });
  }
  res.status(201).json(items.create(r.data));
});
```

Define an `AppError` class carrying `status` and `code`, throw it anywhere, and map it in the one error middleware. Business code never touches `res`.

Why the others are wrong: a `null` carries no reason, so routes guess the status and the shape drifts; per-route handling duplicates code and produces inconsistent error formats; and swallowing errors hides failures and returns wrong data as if it were correct.

```js
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(status, code, message) { super(message); this.status = status; this.code = code; this.expose = true; }
}
throw new AppError(404, "ITEM_NOT_FOUND", "Item not found");
```

Logs as JSON objects with fields (`level`, `time`, `msg`, `requestId`, `userId`, `durationMs`) instead of free text. Machines can filter, aggregate and alert on them; one request can be traced across lines and services.

Why the others are wrong: splitting by level is about routing, the lines are still free text; logging inside transactions loses the log when the transaction rolls back and adds load to the database; and free-text lines need fragile parsing, while JSON loggers like Pino are among the fastest options.

```js
import pino from "pino";
const logger = pino();
logger.info({ requestId, orderId }, "order created");
```

Do not call `next()`; send a response, or call `next(err)` to jump to the error handler.

Why the others are wrong: Express has no `next(false)` (it has `next('route')` and `next('router')`, which skip to other routes); Express ignores a middleware's return value; and calling `next()` after responding lets later handlers try to respond again, which fails with "headers already sent".

```js
const requireAuth = (req, res, next) => {
  if (!req.headers.authorization) return res.status(401).json({ error: "unauthenticated" });
  next();
};
```

Use `res.locals` (request-scoped) or attach a typed property such as `req.user`. For deep call stacks without passing parameters, use `AsyncLocalStorage` (request id, tenant id).

Why the others are wrong: `process.env` is one shared object for the whole process; Node interleaves many requests on one thread, so module-level state leaks between concurrent requests; and a response cookie is not visible on the current request, while `globalThis` is shared by every request.

```js
import { AsyncLocalStorage } from "node:async_hooks";
export const ctx = new AsyncLocalStorage();
app.use((req, res, next) => ctx.run({ requestId: crypto.randomUUID() }, next));
```

Read `process.env` once at startup in a single config module, validate it, freeze it, and import the config object everywhere. Do not scatter `process.env.X` through handlers.

Why the others are wrong: committing connection strings leaks secrets and ties config to builds; a running process's environment doesn't change from outside, and scattered reads skip validation and fail late; and per-environment branches drift apart and make every deploy a merge.

```js
const Env = z.object({ DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(), PORT: z.coerce.number().default(3000) });
export const config = Object.freeze(Env.parse(process.env));
```

Environment variables need a restart. For file or remote config: watch the file (`fs.watch`) or listen for `SIGHUP`, validate the new config, then atomically swap the reference. Feature flags and a config service avoid restarts altogether.

Why the others are wrong: `SIGTERM` asks the process to shut down, and Node reloads nothing by itself; a process's environment is copied at start and outside changes don't reach it; and clearing the module cache re-runs the module but `process.env` stays the same (and ESM has no `require.cache`).

```js
let config = load();
process.on("SIGHUP", () => { try { config = load(); } catch (e) { logger.error(e); } });
```

Constructor injection passes dependencies once when the object or factory is created; method injection passes them per call. JS usually uses factory functions or class constructors; method injection fits things that vary per call (a transaction, a request context).

Why the others are wrong: monkey-patching is a testing hack, not injection; a factory is a plain function taking its dependencies, no decorators or container needed, and method injection passes per call, not once; and the definitions are swapped.

```js
const createOrderService = ({ repo, mailer }) => ({
  async place(order, tx) { await repo.save(order, tx); await mailer.send(order); },
});
```

Use `pino-http` or `morgan`. Log method, path, status, duration, request id, and user id. Redact secrets (`authorization`, passwords, tokens) and avoid logging bodies by default (privacy and volume); sample or log bodies only for failures.

Why the others are wrong: keeping everything forever is a privacy and cost problem and not a logging pipeline; full objects leak tokens, passwords and personal data and are unstructured; and status, duration and request id are what make request logs useful for debugging.

```js
import pinoHttp from "pino-http";
app.use(pinoHttp({ redact: ["req.headers.authorization"] }));
```

Write a factory that takes dependencies and returns the middleware. This keeps it testable and avoids globals.

Why the others are wrong: globals hide dependencies and make tests interfere with each other; Express passes `(req, res, next)` and has no dependency container; and modules are cached, so a per-request `import()` returns the same instance while adding overhead, and it still can't be swapped in tests.

```js
const rateLimit = ({ store, limit }) => async (req, res, next) => {
  if (await store.hit(req.ip) > limit) return res.sendStatus(429);
  next();
};
app.use(rateLimit({ store: redisStore, limit: 100 }));
```
